  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist in Kibana.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-a5e55c10 Web logs overview`**\n- ID: `eff-a5e55c10-web-logs-overview-d655546638a6`, default space, last 7 days.\n- Six panels:\n  - Total requests (count)\n  - Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n  - Average response bytes (average of `bytes`)\n  - Requests over time (line, date histogram on `timestamp`)\n  - Responses by status code (bar, top 10 of `response.keyword`)\n  - Top requested extensions (pie, top 10 of `extension.keyword`)\n\n**Alert rule: `EFF-a5e55c10 5xx spike`**\n- ID: `4fcd71f1-9eb5-4cd1-8b2c-3a2ba94e3274`, type `.es-query`, runs every 1m, enabled. Status is `ok`.\n- Counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_logs` over the last 5 minutes where `response.keyword` matches `5??`, and fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- I tagged it `EFF-a5e55c10` and did not add an action connector, so it records alerts but sends no notifications.\n\n**Data exploration**\n- The data view has the fields I used: `timestamp` (time field), `clientip`, `bytes`, `response`/`response.keyword`, `extension.keyword`, `url.keyword`, `host.keyword`, `geo.dest`, and `agent.keyword`.\n- I could not run ES|QL or aggregation queries against the data. The `_query` and internal search endpoints return 404/400 on this instance, so I did not confirm the actual status-code values or 5xx volume. The `5??` wildcard assumes `response.keyword` holds the status as a string, which the field type suggests but I did not verify. The \"Responses by status code\" panel will show whether it does.",
